window.TIBER_SEARCH = [{"title": "ADA Authorized Lab — Operational & Business Plan", "category": "Overview", "url": "readme.html", "lang": "en", "text": "ADA Authorized Lab — Operational & Business Plan Objective. Stand up a new business line: become an App Defense Alliance Authorized Lab able to perform and validate assessments across all four ADA security profiles — CASA , MASA , DASA , and Cloud Config — and operate it as a profitable, investable direction for a security company. Starting position. Greenfield: no ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation yet . This plan therefore carries the full accreditation track in parallel with the ADA/TrustCB authorization track. ⚠️ Accuracy note. Figures for fees, timelines and the exact proficiency-evaluation mechanics are market estimates and must be confirmed with TrustCB and the chosen accreditation body before commitment. Every such item is tagged VERIFY in the text. Primary sources are indexed in reference/official-sources.md . --- How to read this If you are… Start here --- --- An investor / exec deciding whether to fund this business-case/00-executive-business-case.md → business-case/financial-model.md → business-case/market-sizing.md The program lead running delivery OPERATIONAL-PLAN.md → program-plan/phases-and-timeline.md A quality / accreditation owner accreditation/iso17025-roadmap.md A technical assessor delivery/casa-methodology.md and the per-profile briefs in ada-authorization/ --- Repository map --- The one-paragraph version To sell ADA assessments you must be an Authorized Lab . ADA does not authorize labs itself — it delegates to the certification body TrustCB , which requires every lab to be ISO/IEC 17025-accredited and to pass a proficiency / trial evaluation for each profile it wants to offer. The official path is five steps: petition TrustCB → begin ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation → complete a trial evaluation under TrustCB oversight → complete the ISO/IEC 17025 audit → maintain the authorization . From a standing start the critical path is the ISO/IEC 17025 build-out , which can and should run in parallel with building the CASA/MASA delivery capability and the trial evaluations. The commercial thesis: ADA/CASA is a recurring, annually-revalidated, Google-mandated compliance spend with only ~10 global authorized labs — a scarce, defensible, high-retention position that pairs naturally with an existing pentest practice. See OPERATIONAL-PLAN.md for the full plan."}, {"title": "Master Operational Plan — Becoming an ADA Authorized Lab", "category": "Overview", "url": "operational-plan.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Master Operational Plan — Becoming an ADA Authorized Lab Owner: Program Lead · Status: Draft v0.1 · Date: 2026-10-27 · Review cycle: monthly --- 1. Executive summary We will build and certify a new App Defense Alliance Authorized Lab offering CASA, MASA, DASA and Cloud Config assessments. Authorization is controlled by the certification body TrustCB and is gated on ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation plus a per-profile proficiency / trial evaluation . - Why: ADA/CASA is a Google-mandated, annually revalidated security assessment for apps using sensitive OAuth scopes and for Google Workspace/Play distribution. Demand is recurring and growing, supply is scarce , and the work is adjacent to an existing penetration-testing capability — low marginal cost, high retention. - What it takes: a quality-management system, accredited scope, trained assessors, an evidence/automation pipeline, and successful trial evaluations — ~12–18 months VERIFY from a zero ISO-17025 start to first authorized profile, with CASA first . - The ask : see program-plan/budget-and-financials.md — a setup investment covering accreditation, headcount ramp and tooling, reaching operating break-even within ~Year 2 on a modest assessment volume. This is positioned as an investable business direction , not just a certification project — the strategic case lives in strategy/ . --- 2. Goal, scope, and definition of done Goal. Hold a valid ADA Authorized Lab authorization and deliver paid, ADA-recognized assessments at commercial scale. In scope. All four profiles, sequenced: 1. CASA 2. MASA 3. DASA and Cloud Config Out of scope . Running our own non-ADA product certifications; building an accreditation body; geographic expansion beyond the initial accredited scope. Definition of done . - ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation covering the profile's test methods is live. - TrustCB trial evaluation for the profile is passed . - TrustCB authorization certificate is issued and the lab is listed by ADA. - At least one paying assessment delivered end-to-end with a valid Letter of Validation. - Surveillance / revalidation calendar is in place. --- 3. The two parallel tracks Authorization sits at the intersection of two workstreams that must run concurrently , not sequentially — the ISO track is the long pole, so start it on day one. The 5 official ADA steps are: 1. Petition to become an Authorized Lab 2. Begin the ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation process 3. Complete a trial evaluation with TrustCB oversight 4. Complete the ISO/IEC 17025 audit 5. Ongoing maintenance of the authorization Full detail: ada-authorization/overview."}, {"title": "Executive Business Case — ADA Authorized Lab", "category": "Business case", "url": "business-case-00-executive-business-case.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Executive Business Case — ADA Authorized Lab Investor-oriented summary. One page of signal; the models behind every number are linked. Figures are modelled estimates unless marked confirmed — see the honesty box at the end. --- The opportunity Google requires third-party apps using sensitive/restricted OAuth scopes to pass an annual security assessment — CASA — performed by an authorized lab ; mobile apps seeking Google Play's trust badge need MASA . Self-assessment is no longer accepted for restricted scopes. Yet only ~10 labs worldwide are authorized to perform this work. That is a mandated, recurring, supply-constrained market. The business: become an ADA Authorized Lab and monetize a high-retention annuity behind a scarce license — built on existing penetration-testing capability. Why it's investable 1. Mandated demand — compliance, not discretionary spend. 2. Recurring by design — annual revalidation → renewing clients, compounding base. 3. Scarce, defensible license — the ISO/IEC 17025 + TrustCB barrier that's hard for us is the same wall that keeps the 11th–20th competitors out. 4. Capital-light, high-margin services — automation drives margin and capacity. 5. Low execution risk — productizing web/mobile/cloud pentests we already do, under a QMS. The numbers Year 1 Year 3 Year 5 --- --- --- --- Revenue $0.14M $1.5M $4.4M EBITDA −$0.46M −$0.19M +$0.88M Active client base 40 476 1,536 - Break-even: ~Year 4 . - Funding ask: ~$1.8–2.0M . - Payback: ~Year 6 ; illustrative gross MOIC ~8–11× on a ~3–4× revenue exit. Scenario spread ): Scenario Y5 revenue Y5 EBITDA Break-even Illustrative MOIC --- --- --- --- --- Conservative $2.0M $0.07M ~Y5 ~3–4× Realistic $4.4M $0.88M ~Y4 ~8–11× Optimistic $8.6M $3.18M ~Y3 ~20×+ Pricing we're modelling against - CASA: AL1 ~$500–$3,000; AL2 ~$4,500–$8,000 . - MASA: AL1 ~$500 , AL2 ~$3,000–6,000 . - Full map + sources: competitor-pricing.md . Where we win vs. the ~10 incumbents - Automation-first → faster turnaround + lower cost than manual incumbents . - Pentest depth → credible AL2 where lighter labs cluster at AL1. - Multi-profile one-stop and EN/UA/CEE-EU regional reach. - Detail: ../strategy/competitive-landscape.md . Use of funds ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation , TrustCB petition + trial evaluations, a small senior quality/assessor team, and the evidence-automation platform — through CASA go-live and first revenue , with runway to the conservative break-even. Breakdown: financial-model.md §3–4 . The ask ~$1.8–2.0M to cross a regulatory-grade barrier only ~10 firms worldwide have crossed, and sit on the renewing side of"}, {"title": "Competitor & Market Pricing (researched)", "category": "Business case", "url": "business-case-competitor-pricing.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Competitor & Market Pricing Research date: 2026-10-27. Confirmed data = published by the lab, Google, or a named buyer. Indicative = third-party blog/aggregator. There is no official Google/ADA price list — all labs quote privately, so treat these as a pricing map, not a tariff . Get written quotes before modelling final numbers. Sources at the bottom. CASA — Cloud Application Security Assessment Lab / source AL1 AL2 Confidence --- --- --- --- Google / ADA stated range — — $500 – $4,500 overall · Indicative TAC Security ~$540 basic → ~$1,800 premium ~$4,500 Indicative NCC Group ~$1,000 – $1,200 start ~$5,000 – $8,000 Indicative Prescient Security ~$1,000 – $1,200 ~$5,000 – $8,000 Indicative Bishop Fox ~$1,500 start Indicative Leviathan Security $3,000 → $6,000 ~$5,000 – $8,000 Indicative Independent assessors $3,000+ seen — Buyer-reported Takeaways: - AL1 is a wide, competitive band — commoditizing at the low end , premium at full-service firms. - AL2 is the value tier and is where pentest-grade firms differentiate. - Everything recurs annually — the renewal is the real prize. MASA — Mobile Application Security Assessment Source AL1 AL2 Confidence --- --- --- --- Google MASA FAQ ~$500 ~$3,000 – $6,000 Confirmed Secusy $499 $2,999 Confirmed NowSecure / NCC / Bishop Fox authorized labs, no public list price same Confirmed status, price on quote The Google MASA FAQ price band is the single most authoritative price anchor we have: AL1 ≈ $500, AL2 ≈ $3–6k. Note the ~$500 TrustCB certification fee embedded in AL2 — a real per-assessment scheme cost to model in COGS. DASA / Cloud Config / AI Agents & Tools No reliable public price points. Modelling assumptions : - DASA : ~$600 – $1,200. - Cloud Config : ~$2,000 – $4,000 incremental. - AI Agents & Tools : ~$5,000 – $10,000. VERIFY whether this is yet a formal ADA authorized-lab profile — see ../ada-authorization/ai-agents-profile.md . Cross-ecosystem price context Ecosystem Program Who pays / price Fit for our lab --- --- --- --- Google CASA / MASA Developer pays the lab Core — our authorized-lab revenue Microsoft Microsoft 365 App Compliance / Certification Historically free to developer ; maps to SOC2/ISO/PCI, not ASVS/CASA Adjacent — different assessor panel; VERIFY current cost model & lab onboarding Meta Data Protection Assessment / Data Use Checkup Third-party assessment for Graph-API data access Adjacent — different scheme; VERIFY lab eligibility Important for the investor case: the confirmed, developer-paid, lab-authorized revenue is the Google/ADA path . Microsoft and Meta are optional adjacencies that "}, {"title": "Market Sizing — TAM / SAM / SOM", "category": "Business case", "url": "business-case-market-sizing.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Market Sizing — TAM / SAM / SOM Method note. No public figure exists for the number of CASA/MASA-obligated apps, so this is a transparent bottom-up model with explicitly flagged assumptions . Every ASSUMPTION is a dial you can change. Confirmed anchors: the requirement is real, annual, lab-only and pricing is mapped in competitor-pricing.md . Replace assumptions with sourced data before investor use. What creates an obligated app - Google CASA: apps using sensitive/restricted OAuth scopes with a meaningful user base must complete an annual CASA assessment by an authorized lab. Restricted scopes + higher risk → AL2 ; sensitive → AL1 . - Google MASA: Android apps seeking the Play \"independent security review\" badge. - Both recur annually → the installed base compounds. TAM — Total Addressable Market Bottom-up: obligated apps × annual price. Segment Obligated apps ASSUMPTION Avg annual price Annual TAM --- --- --- --- Google CASA 40,000 $1,500 $60.0M Google CASA 12,000 $5,500 $66.0M Google MASA 30,000 $1,500 $45.0M Google ecosystem subtotal ~$171M/yr Microsoft 365 / Meta adjacencies — excluded from core Core ADA TAM ~$150–200M/yr The $150–200M/yr core TAM is the defensible number. Analyst \"app security testing market\" figures are a different, broader market — don't conflate them with the CASA/MASA-specific annuity. ASSUMPTION: obligated-app counts are planning estimates; the ranges, not the point values, are what matter. SAM — Serviceable Addressable Market The slice we can realistically serve given language, region, profile scope and positioning: - Profiles we'll offer: CASA + MASA first . - Positioning: automation-first, competitive AL1, strong AL2, EN+UA+CEE/EU reach. - SAM assumption: ~ 20–25% of core TAM is realistically serviceable by a mid-tier, automation-led lab → ~$30–45M/yr . SOM — Serviceable Obtainable Market Capture is supply-constrained , not demand-constrained — only ~10 labs exist, so the binding limit is how many assessments we can staff , not whether demand exists ). Horizon Capture of SAM ASSUMPTION Implied revenue --- --- --- Year 3 ~3–5% of SAM ~$1.5M Year 5 ~10–15% of SAM ~$4–5M → consistent with the realistic revenue forecast built independently from volumes in financial-model.md . The two methods triangulate to the same order of magnitude , which is the point. Cross-ecosystem demand Ecosystem Demand engine Counts toward authorized-lab revenue? --- --- --- Google CASA + MASA YES — core, confirmed Microsoft M365 App Compliance / Certification Adjacency — different scheme, historically Microsoft-funded; VERIFY Meta Data Protection Asse"}, {"title": "Financial Model — 5-Year Projections, Costs, Profitability & ROI", "category": "Business case", "url": "business-case-financial-model.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Financial Model — 5-Year Projections, Costs, Profitability & ROI Status: illustrative model, not a forecast. Every output below is derived from the assumptions table — change an assumption, change the result. Pricing anchors are researched ); volumes, costs, margins and retention are ASSUMPTIONS to be replaced with quotes and a bottom-up staffing plan. All figures USD , thousands unless noted. The three failure modes that break these numbers are tracked in ../program-plan/risk-register.md . --- 1. Core assumptions Assumption Conservative Realistic Optimistic Basis --- --- --- --- --- New ACV $3,200 $3,500 $4,000 Researched price map; mix of AL1/AL2/profiles Renewal ACV $2,100 $2,300 $2,600 ~65% of new Annual retention 80% 85% 90% Annuity assumption Gross margin Y1→Y5 40→57% 45→65% 48→68% Automation lowers assessor-days/job Go-live ~Mo 16 ~Mo 15 ~Mo 13 ISO 17025 critical path Profiles by Y5 CASA+MASA +Cloud Config +AI Agents Sequencing Capacity reality: revenue is supply-constrained . Annual capacity ≈ FTE assessors × ~180 billable days ÷ assessor-days-per-job . Volume ramps below are staffed against this — see §5. --- 2. Revenue build Active base compounds: active = new + retention × active . Conservative Y1 Y2 Y3 Y4 Y5 --- --- --- --- --- --- New assessments 20 80 160 260 380 Renewals 0 16 77 190 360 Active base 20 96 237 450 740 Revenue 64 290 674 1,231 1,972 Realistic Y1 Y2 Y3 Y4 Y5 --- --- --- --- --- --- New assessments 40 150 320 520 750 Renewals 0 34 156 405 786 Active base 40 184 476 925 1,536 Revenue 140 603 1,479 2,752 4,433 Optimistic Y1 Y2 Y3 Y4 Y5 --- --- --- --- --- --- New assessments 60 240 520 850 1,250 Renewals 0 54 265 706 1,400 Active base 60 294 785 1,556 2,650 Revenue 240 1,100 2,769 5,236 8,640 --- 3. Startup costs — \"cost to cross the moat\" Pre-revenue, ~Months 0–15. VERIFY every line with real quotes. Item Low High Notes --- --- --- --- ISO/IEC 17025 consultant $80k $150k Biggest controllable; de-risks timeline Accreditation body $40k $80k Assessor-days + travel TrustCB petition + trial evals $25k $60k VERIFY fee schedule Evidence/automation platform build $100k $200k Buy scanners, build orchestration layer Legal / entity / impartiality structure $20k $40k Firewall, contracts Launch marketing $30k $60k Partly Y1 opex One-time setup total ~$300k ~$590k Model point: ~$400k Build-phase salaries are not in the table above — they flow through the Year-1 operating loss below, which is why the funding ask sizes to the cumulative cash trough , not just setup capex. --- 4. Operating cost structure Overhead = non-delivery fixed cost . Lean"}, {"title": "Mindmaps & Diagrams", "category": "Business case", "url": "business-case-mindmaps.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Mindmaps & Diagrams Mermaid diagrams. They render in the generated site or any Mermaid viewer. Labels are quoted to avoid parser issues with special characters. 1. The authorization journey 2. Profiles and the standards beneath them 3. The revenue flywheel 4. Cross-ecosystem demand map 5. The investment logic 6. Capacity-constrained growth"}, {"title": "Investment Thesis — ADA Authorized Lab", "category": "Strategy", "url": "strategy-investment-thesis.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Investment Thesis — ADA Authorized Lab A fundable new direction for a security company. All figures illustrative — VERIFY and replace with modelled numbers before any investor conversation. The thesis in one line Own a scarce, regulator-like license that turns a Google-mandated, annually-recurring compliance requirement into a high-retention service annuity — built on top of penetration-testing capability we already have. Why this is attractive to an investor 1. Mandated, non-discretionary demand. CASA is required by Google for apps using sensitive OAuth scopes and for key marketplace surfaces. Buyers don't choose whether to comply — only who validates them. Demand is structural, not sentiment-driven. 2. Recurring revenue by design. Validations are revalidated annually → a validated client is a renewing client. Net revenue retention is inherently high once the base exists. 3. Scarce supply / high barrier to entry. Only ~10 authorized labs globally . The barrier is exactly what makes the position defensible — the same wall we must climb keeps future competitors out. This is a moat , not just a cost. 4. Capital-light, high-gross-margin services. The scarce asset is authorization + trained assessors + automation , not capex. Marginal cost per assessment falls as tooling matures. 5. Capability adjacency / low execution risk. We already run web/API, mobile and cloud pentests . ADA profiles are productized, standardized versions of work we do — so the ramp is re-packaging + accreditation , not R&D from zero. 6. Platform for expansion. One accreditation + lab machinery unlocks four profiles and a credible path to adjacent attestations — land-and-expand within each client. The \"why now\" - Google has tightened and routinized CASA for the OAuth ecosystem; the requirement is maturing from niche to standard ). - The scheme recently moved to the Linux Foundation and to the TrustCB/ISO-17025 lab model , so the authorized-lab roster is still small and forming — early entrants lock in brand and reference clients. - OWASP ASVS 5.0 and MASVS refreshes create a re-mapping cycle that favors labs with modern, automated methodology over incumbents on legacy processes. What we are asking for - Setup capital to fund: ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation , the TrustCB petition and trial evaluations, a small senior assessor + quality team, and an evidence/ automation pipeline — through to first authorized profile and initial revenue. - Runway to ~Year-2 operating break-even on a modest assessment volume. - Indicative sizing and the P&L sketch: ../program-plan/budget-and-financials.md . Re"}, {"title": "Market Opportunity", "category": "Strategy", "url": "strategy-market-opportunity.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Market Opportunity All quantities are illustrative frameworks to be populated with sourced data before use — VERIFY every number with primary research. Demand drivers 1. Google OAuth / restricted-scope apps. Any third-party app requesting sensitive or restricted Google API scopes must complete CASA — and re-complete it annually . The population of such apps is large and growing . 2. Google Play \"Independent security review\" . Mobile developers seek the Play Data- safety badge → recurring MASA demand. 3. Google Workspace Marketplace. Listing/badging has been tied to higher CASA assurance → pulls apps up the assurance ladder . 4. AI-agent explosion. The wave of AI assistants/agents requesting mailbox, document and drive scopes is a net-new, fast-growing cohort of CASA-obligated apps — a timing tailwind. 5. Procurement spillover. CASA/MASA validation increasingly appears in enterprise vendor- security questionnaires as a trust signal beyond the strict Google mandate. Sizing framework Build the model top-down and bottom-up and reconcile: - TAM — all apps globally obligated to CASA/MASA annually × average assessment price. Input needed: count of restricted-scope OAuth apps + Play apps pursuing the badge. VERIFY - SAM — the slice we can serve given language, geography, pricing tier and profile scope . - SOM — realistic capture given ~10 competitors and our assessor capacity. Capacity is the real near-term ceiling ), so SOM is supply-constrained , which is favorable: we can sell out capacity before price competition bites. Because supply is the scarce side, the near-term question is \"how many assessments can we staff?\" more than \"can we find demand?\" . Model capacity first. Pricing context VERIFY Public/vendor sources put third-party CASA engagements in a broad range depending on assurance level and app complexity . Confirm current market pricing from competitor quotes and ADA materials; do not anchor the model on blog figures. Why a new entrant can win now - Roster still forming post-Linux-Foundation move → room for new authorized labs. - Modern, automation-first delivery vs. incumbents' manual processes → better margin and turnaround . - Multilingual / regional positioning can capture underserved segments and regional procurement preferences. - Pentest heritage lets us credibly offer AL2 where lighter competitors cluster at AL1. Segments to target first 1. AI/LLM SaaS requesting Google scopes . 2. Mid-market SaaS with Workspace Marketplace ambitions . 3. Mobile-first products chasing the Play badge — bundle with CASA for web+mobile apps. 4. Agencies / platfor"}, {"title": "Competitive Landscape", "category": "Strategy", "url": "strategy-competitive-landscape.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Competitive Landscape The field is small by design ADA lists only ~10 authorized labs worldwide , including: Bishop Fox, DEKRA, Eydle, Leviathan Security, NCC Group, NetSentries, NowSecure, Prescient Security, TAC Security, ValueMentor . VERIFY the current roster on the ADA Authorized Labs page — it changes. This scarcity is the core asset : the ISO/IEC 17025 + TrustCB barrier that is hard for us to cross is equally hard for the next entrant, so crossing it buys durable positioning. Rough segmentation of incumbents Type Examples Posture Where we compete --- --- --- --- Global assurance / testing giants DEKRA, NCC Group, Bishop Fox Brand, breadth, enterprise accounts; premium price; slower, manual Not on brand — on price/turnaround for SMB–mid-market Specialist security firms NowSecure , Leviathan, Prescient Deep niche Match on quality; win on multi-profile bundling Volume / value labs TAC Security, ValueMentor, NetSentries Cost-competitive, high throughput Win on automation + AL2 depth + region/language Our differentiation 1. Automation-first delivery → faster turnaround and lower cost/assessment than manual incumbents. Turnaround time is a top buyer complaint in this market — a concrete wedge. 2. Pentest heritage → credible AL2. We can do the deep, infrastructure-and-data-storage level, where many competitors cluster at lighter AL1. 3. Multi-profile one-stop shop. CASA + MASA + Cloud Config under one lab = web + mobile + cloud for an app in a single relationship . 4. Regional / multilingual positioning → underserved segments and procurement preferences that the US/UK-centric roster doesn't serve well. 5. Modern standards posture — built directly on current ASVS 5.0 / MASVS, not retrofitted legacy processes. Competitive risks - Incumbent price-matching on commodity AL1 work → defend with automation cost base and move clients to bundled AL2 + Cloud Config . - Roster expansion → compresses scarcity over time; first-mover reference clients and renewals are the hedge. - Brand disadvantage vs. NCC/Bishop Fox for large enterprises → target SMB/mid-market and regional accounts first; build case studies before moving upmarket. Positioning statement \"The fast, automation-driven ADA Authorized Lab for the whole app — web , mobile and cloud — with the depth to deliver the comprehensive AL2 level and the turnaround that growing SaaS teams actually need.\""}, {"title": "Revenue Model & Unit Economics", "category": "Strategy", "url": "strategy-revenue-model.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Revenue Model & Unit Economics All numbers are placeholders / illustrative — replace with sourced, modelled figures. VERIFY market pricing and effort before relying on any of this. Revenue streams 1. CASA assessments — AL1 and AL2, priced by assurance level + app complexity. Annually revalidated → renewing. 2. MASA assessments — mobile, per app, periodically revalidated. 3. DASA — lighter-touch, higher-volume, lower price. 4. Cloud Config — usually bundled with CASA AL2 to raise average deal value. 5. Readiness / pre-assessment advisory — structurally separated from the certifying lab to preserve impartiality . 6. Renewals — the compounding annuity; model net revenue retention explicitly. The independence constraint ISO/IEC 17025 impartiality rules mean the lab cannot certify an application it also remediated/consulted on . Therefore: - Keep \"get-ready\" advisory\" and \"validate\" as separate offerings / teams with a documented firewall, or refer remediation to partners. - This is a feature , not just a limit: it creates a clean partner channel — see partnership-strategy.md . Unit-economics model Per assessment: Levers that move margin: - Automation of ASVS/MASVS evidence collection → fewer assessor days per job ). - Reuse across profiles . - Renewal efficiency — year-2 revalidation of an unchanged app is far cheaper to deliver than a first assessment → renewals carry higher margin than new logos. Capacity model Because authorized-lab supply is scarce, capacity, not demand, caps near-term revenue : Plan hires against a capacity target, not a demand target. Automation raises capacity without linear headcount — the scalability story for investors. Illustrative ramp VERIFY Year 1 Year 2 Year 3 --- --- --- --- Authorized profiles CASA CASA + MASA + DASA + Cloud Config New assessments small pilot cohort growing scaling Renewal base — Year-1 cohort renews Y1+Y2 cohorts renew Economics investment phase approach break-even profitable, compounding Tie these to the P&L in ../program-plan/budget-and-financials.md . Pricing strategy - Enter at competitive AL1 price to win reference logos and renewals. - Make margin on AL2 + Cloud Config bundles . - Price renewals to retain rather than to maximize first-year take."}, {"title": "Partnership & Channel Strategy", "category": "Strategy", "url": "strategy-partnership-strategy.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Partnership & Channel Strategy The authorized-lab license is most valuable when it sits at the center of a channel rather than selling only direct. Partnerships also carry the investor-relevant narrative: a defensible position with multiple distribution and exit paths. 1. ADA / TrustCB - TrustCB is the gatekeeper and ongoing certification body — treat it as a managed relationship . Stay close to catch methodology/fee changes early. - ADA / Google ecosystem — being listed as an authorized lab is itself a demand channel . Optimize that listing and any co-marketing. 2. Consultancy / remediation partners Because the lab cannot both fix and certify the same app, there is a natural two-sided flow: - Security consultancies & dev agencies refer clients to us for the independent validation they cannot self-provide. - We refer remediation back to them . - Formalize as a referral/partner program with revenue share. This turns a compliance constraint into a lead engine . 3. Platform & aggregator channels - SaaS platforms / marketplaces whose developers face CASA → bulk/volume referral deals. - Cloud/dev platforms and compliance-automation vendors → integrate \"get CASA-validated\" into their workflow; co-sell. - AI-agent platforms — the fast-growing cohort needing Google-scope validation; early partnerships capture the wave. 4. Audit / assurance firm alliances - Firms doing SOC 2 / ISO 27001 can bundle CASA/MASA; mapping pre-existing certs into CASA requirements is an explicit CASA feature → joint offerings. 5. The M&A / strategic angle - Authorized-lab status is scarce and acquirable . Several incumbents on the roster are the kind of acquirers that buy licenses + trained teams rather than build them. - A profitable, multi-profile authorized lab with a renewing client base is a clean bolt-on acquisition → credible strategic exit, in addition to the standalone annuity. Partnership priorities 1. TrustCB relationship — mandatory, from day one. 2. Remediation-partner referral program — launch at CASA go-live . 3. 1–2 platform/aggregator deals — once delivery is proven, for volume. 4. Assurance-firm bundles — as the brand matures."}, {"title": "ADA Authorization — the scheme, profiles and official steps", "category": "ADA & profiles", "url": "ada-authorization-overview.html", "lang": "en", "text": "ADA Authorization — the scheme, profiles and official steps What the App Defense Alliance is The App Defense Alliance is an industry body that runs standardized app-security assessment programs. Its assessments gate real commercial outcomes — most importantly Google's CASA requirement for apps using sensitive/restricted OAuth scopes and listings/badges on Google surfaces. Crucially for us: ADA does not authorize labs directly. It delegates lab authorization to an external certification body, TrustCB , which: - manages the lab authorization process, - reviews the labs' profile assessments , and - issues the final authorization certificate . The four profiles we will offer Profile Full name What it assesses Basis --- --- --- --- CASA Cloud Application Security Assessment Web apps & APIs, their deployment infra and data storage OWASP ASVS MASA Mobile Application Security Assessment Android / iOS mobile apps OWASP MASVS / MASTG DASA Developer-Attested Security Assessment Developer-attested security posture ADA scheme Cloud Config Cloud Configuration Cloud environment configuration posture ADA scheme VERIFY Profile names and exact scope evolve. The ADA Authorized Labs page currently lists \"CASA, DASA, MASA, and Cloud Config\" profiles. Confirm the precise current catalogue and each profile's methodology document with TrustCB at petition time. Per-profile briefs: casa-profile.md · masa-profile.md · dasa-profile.md · cloud-config-profile.md . A lab is authorized per profile — each requires its own proficiency/trial evaluation, so we sequence them . The two hard requirements for any lab 1. ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation — the general requirement for the competence of testing and calibration laboratories. This is the gating prerequisite and the longest-lead item. See ../accreditation/iso17025-roadmap.md . 2. Proficiency evaluation per profile — a TrustCB-overseen trial evaluation proving the lab can correctly execute that profile's methodology before it is authorized to sell it. The 5 official steps 1. Petition to become an Authorized Lab — submit the \"Request to become an Authorized Lab\" , which routes to TrustCB's ADA certification intake. 2. Begin the ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation process — start the QMS build and engage an accreditation body . 3. Complete a trial evaluation with TrustCB oversight — perform a supervised assessment for the target profile ; TrustCB reviews the output for competence. 4. Complete the ISO/IEC 17025 audit — the accreditation body's on-site/remote assessment of the lab's competence and QMS; on success, accreditation is granted. 5. Ongoing "}, {"title": "CASA — Cloud Application Security Assessment", "category": "ADA & profiles", "url": "ada-authorization-casa-profile.html", "lang": "en", "text": "CASA — Cloud Application Security Assessment What it is CASA is ADA's security assessment for cloud/web applications and APIs . Its most important commercial driver: Google requires CASA for third-party apps that access sensitive or restricted OAuth scopes and for certain marketplace/distribution surfaces. It is revalidated annually , which makes it a recurring compliance spend rather than a one-off. The standard underneath: OWASP ASVS CASA requirements are derived from the OWASP Application Security Verification Standard . - Historically CASA aligned to ASVS v4.0.x , with a defined subset of CASA requirements . VERIFY current count. - OWASP has since published ASVS 5.0 . The ADA notes CASA requirements have been updated. VERIFY which ASVS version the current CASA requirements track, and re-map our method when it changes ). Not every requirement applies to every app; some can be satisfied by pre-existing certifications mapped to CASA . The developer must pass all applicable CASA requirements. Assurance levels ADA's tiering page now describes two levels, both labelled \"Lab Tested – Lab Verified\" : Level Formerly Who tests Scope --- --- --- --- AL1 ~Tier 2 Authorized lab verifies evidence Lab tested / lab verified against applicable CASA requirements AL2 ~Tier 3 Authorized lab tests directly Comprehensive — the application plus its deployment infrastructure and user-data storage locations - The framework user — not the developer — determines the required level , based on data sensitivity, user counts per data type, and risk indicators. - A Google Workspace Marketplace badge has been associated with the higher level . VERIFY current badge/level mapping. - Legacy \"Tier 1/2/3\" wording still appears in older certificates and vendor pages; treat AL1/AL2 as current. The deliverable A successful assessment yields a Letter of Validation issued by the authorized lab, with roughly one-year validity → the client must re-engage annually. This annuity is central to the revenue model. Why CASA is our first profile - Largest, most recurring demand of the four profiles . - Closest to an existing web/API pentest capability → fastest competency ramp. - Clear, standards-based methodology → tractable trial evaluation. See the delivery workflow in ../delivery/casa-methodology.md ."}, {"title": "MASA — Mobile Application Security Assessment", "category": "ADA & profiles", "url": "ada-authorization-masa-profile.html", "lang": "en", "text": "MASA — Mobile Application Security Assessment What it is MASA is ADA's security assessment for mobile applications . It lets developers display an \"Independent security review\" badge on their Google Play Data safety section and supports Play trust signals. Like CASA it is periodically revalidated . The standard underneath: OWASP MASVS / MASTG MASA is based on the OWASP Mobile Application Security Verification Standard and the Mobile Application Security Testing Guide . Assessment covers a defined subset of MASVS controls . VERIFY the current MASA requirement set and MASTG test mapping with TrustCB. Why MASA is our fast-follow This is a strategic synergy , not a cold start: - We already have a mobile security testing capability and playbooks . - The MASVS/MASTG methodology is well-documented and automatable. - Shared QMS, evidence pipeline and assessor pool with CASA → low marginal cost to add. Deliverable & cadence An independent assessment resulting in a validation the developer can reference on Play. Validity and revalidation cadence per ADA/Play policy — VERIFY current validity window. Sequencing note Add MASA immediately after CASA go-live , reusing the same accreditation scope extension and a MASA-specific proficiency/trial evaluation with TrustCB. Target: within the same ~Months 14–20 window )."}, {"title": "DASA — Developer-Attested Security Assessment", "category": "ADA & profiles", "url": "ada-authorization-dasa-profile.html", "lang": "en", "text": "DASA — Developer-Attested Security Assessment What it is DASA is an ADA profile oriented around developer attestation of security posture, with lab involvement in validating the attestation rather than a full independent test. It is a lighter-touch, higher-volume profile relative to CASA AL2. VERIFY DASA is the least publicly documented of the four profiles. Confirm its exact definition, the lab's role, the evidence/attestation model, and its proficiency-evaluation requirements directly from the TrustCB scheme documents before planning delivery. Why we add it later - Lower complexity per engagement → attractive volume/throughput play once the lab machinery exists. - Reuses the CASA requirement base and evidence pipeline. - Good entry-level / upsell funnel : developers who start with an attested assessment can be moved up to CASA AL1/AL2 as their risk level rises. Delivery model - Intake of developer-provided evidence/attestation. - Lab review against the applicable requirement set. - Validation output per the ADA scheme. Flesh out once the Evaluation Methodology document is obtained and ../reference/official-sources.md )."}, {"title": "Cloud Config — Cloud Configuration (review) profile", "category": "ADA & profiles", "url": "ada-authorization-cloud-config-profile.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Cloud Config — Cloud Configuration profile What it is The Cloud Config profile assesses the security configuration of an application's cloud environment , complementing the application-layer focus of CASA. It is the natural companion to a CASA AL2 engagement, whose scope already reaches deployment infrastructure and data storage . VERIFY Confirm the exact Cloud Config methodology, the cloud providers in scope , the benchmark basis , and its proficiency evaluation with TrustCB. Why we add it last - Bundles with CASA AL2 → increases average engagement value without new client acquisition. - Leverages existing cloud-pentest / cloud-config-review capability — strong internal synergy. - Lowest incremental demand as a standalone profile, so it rides on CASA rather than leading. Delivery model - Read-only access / configuration export of the target cloud environment. - Review against the profile's control set + relevant CIS/provider benchmarks. - Findings + validation output per the ADA scheme. Internal synergy Map our existing cloud offensive/defensive tooling and checklists to the Cloud Config control set when the methodology document is obtained — this is largely a packaging exercise over capability we already have."}, {"title": "AI Agents & Tools (emerging profile)", "category": "ADA & profiles", "url": "ada-authorization-ai-agents-profile.html", "lang": "en", "text": "AI Agents & Tools VERIFY — status caveat. As of 2026-10 the ADA Authorized-Labs page formally lists CASA, DASA, MASA, and Cloud Config . \"AI Agents & Tools\" is an emerging assessment area, not confirmed as a distinct authorized-lab profile. Treat it as a strategic adjacency / future profile , and confirm its existence, scheme and proficiency requirements with TrustCB before planning delivery or counting its revenue as authorized-lab revenue. Why it matters anyway The wave of AI agents, LLM tools and copilots that request sensitive OAuth scopes is the fastest-growing cohort of CASA-obligated apps . Whether assessed under CASA or a future dedicated AI profile, this cohort is a major demand driver — and it plays directly to in-house capability . What an AI-agent assessment would cover - Standard CASA/ASVS application controls for the hosting app/API, plus AI-specific risks: prompt injection, tool/function-call abuse, excessive agency, insecure output handling, data exfiltration via the agent, RAG/data-source poisoning, over-broad scope/permission use. - Alignment with emerging standards: OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications / Agentic AI , and any ADA/Google AI assessment criteria as they publish. Strategic positioning - Differentiator: few of the ~10 incumbent labs lead on AI-agent security; an automation-+- AI-red-team-native lab can own this niche early . - Premium pricing: complex, high-value work . - Timing: position now , formalize when ADA/TrustCB defines the profile. Sequencing Treat as profile 5 / future — build the methodology and market presence while CASA/MASA generate revenue; convert to an authorized profile the moment the scheme supports it. In the financial model it sits in the optimistic scenario and the Year 3+ upside, not the conservative base. See ../business-case/financial-model.md ."}, {"title": "ISO/IEC 17025 Accreditation Roadmap", "category": "Accreditation", "url": "accreditation-iso17025-roadmap.html", "lang": "en", "text": "ISO/IEC 17025 Accreditation Roadmap The gating prerequisite. Start on day one — it is the critical path to authorization. Why ISO/IEC 17025 ADA/TrustCB require every authorized lab to be ISO/IEC 17025-accredited . ISO/IEC 17025 is the international standard for the competence of testing and calibration laboratories . It proves, through an independent accreditation body, that the lab produces technically valid results under a controlled management system with demonstrated impartiality and competence . For a security-testing lab, accreditation is granted for a defined scope — the specific test methods we are competent to perform. Choosing the accreditation body Accreditation is issued by a national/regional accreditation body that is a signatory to the ILAC MRA , so the result is internationally recognized. Examples by region : - EU/EEA: the national accreditation body — e.g. SNAS , DAkkS , etc. - UK: UKAS. US: A2LA, ANAB. Others per ILAC. VERIFY Confirm with TrustCB whether they require a specific accreditation body or accept any ILAC-MRA signatory, and whether a particular scope wording is expected for ADA profiles. Also confirm domicile — this drives which body applies. What accreditation requires 1. Define the scope — the exact test methods . 2. Build the Quality Management System covering ISO/IEC 17025 clauses, including: - Impartiality & confidentiality . - Personnel competence — training, authorization, and competency records per assessor. - Document & record control ; control of the assessment process. - Method validation / verification — evidence that our CASA method reliably produces valid results. - Handling of test items , equipment/tooling control, and measurement/decision traceability . - Nonconforming work, corrective action, complaints & appeals. - Internal audits and management review . 3. Operate the system long enough to generate records . 4. Internal audit + management review — at least one full cycle before the external audit. 5. External assessment by the accreditation body — document review + on-site/remote witnessed assessment of real evaluations; close any findings . 6. Accreditation granted for the scope; thereafter surveillance and scope extensions when we add profiles. Indicative timeline VERIFY From a zero start , a realistic range is ~9–18 months to first accreditation, driven by how fast the QMS is built and how much operating history is required before the assessor audit. Running method-build and dry-runs in parallel compresses this. Indicative cost drivers VERIFY - ISO consultant — the biggest controllable cost. - Accredita"}, {"title": "CASA Delivery Methodology (working draft)", "category": "Delivery", "url": "delivery-casa-methodology.html", "lang": "en", "text": "CASA Delivery Methodology This is our interpretation of how a CASA engagement runs. The authoritative method is ADA's Evaluation Methodology + CASA requirements documents — reconcile this file against them once obtained, and keep it a controlled document inside the ISO/IEC 17025 QMS. VERIFY everything marked below. Inputs - Target application + API scope; for AL2 also deployment infrastructure and user-data storage locations . - The applicable CASA requirement set . - Any pre-existing certifications the client wants mapped to CASA requirements. - Access: source/scan access, test environment, credentials as needed per assurance level. Assurance-level workflows AL1 — lab tested / lab verified 1. Client runs the required automated scans producing evidence per test case. 2. Lab reviews and validates the evidence against each applicable CASA requirement. 3. Lab resolves gaps / requests remediation evidence. 4. Lab issues the Letter of Validation when all applicable requirements pass. AL2 — comprehensive 1. Lab performs active testing of the application and its deployment infrastructure and data storage — i.e. a structured pentest mapped to the CASA/ASVS control set. 2. Lab validates automated + manual findings against every applicable requirement. 3. Remediation loop; re-test. 4. Letter of Validation on full pass. The ASVS-mapped assessment core For each applicable requirement : Step Action Record --- --- --- 1 Determine applicability to this app applicability note 2 Collect evidence evidence artifact ID 3 Verify against the requirement's pass criteria pass/fail + rationale 4 Decision with traceability decision record 5 On nonconform → remediation request → re-verify corrective-action log ASVS categories to cover : encoding/output, validation & sanitization, authentication, session management, access control, cryptography, error handling & logging, data protection, communications/TLS, configuration, business logic, API/web-service, and others per the current standard. Decision rule & deliverable - Pass: all applicable CASA requirements conform → Letter of Validation . - Record everything for traceability: ISO/IEC 17025 requires defensible, reproducible decisions and retained evidence. QMS integration - This method is a controlled document . - Only authorized, competency-recorded assessors may execute it. - Each engagement generates records that feed both the client deliverable and ISO/IEC 17025 auditability. - Impartiality: no assessor may validate an app they built/consulted on. Reuse of existing capability Our web/API pentest playbooks map directly onto AL2"}, {"title": "Toolchain & Automation", "category": "Delivery", "url": "delivery-toolchain-and-automation.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Toolchain & Automation Automation is the margin and capacity lever ): fewer assessor-days per assessment, faster turnaround than manual incumbents, and capacity growth without linear headcount. Build the evidence pipeline early so it doubles as QMS operating records and trial-evaluation rehearsal. Principles - Evidence-first: every CASA/ASVS requirement maps to a repeatable evidence collector with a stored, ID'd artifact. - Traceable & reproducible: ISO/IEC 17025 demands defensible, repeatable results — automation must log what ran, against what, when, by whom . - Modular by standard version: isolate the ASVS/MASVS mapping so a version bump is a config change, not a rewrite. - Human-in-the-loop decisioning: tools gather evidence; authorized assessors decide pass/fail. Never auto-issue a Letter of Validation. Tool categories Need Category Notes --- --- --- SAST Static analysis / secure code review Feeds many ASVS code-level requirements DAST Dynamic app/API scanning Core AL1 automated evidence; AL2 active testing SCA / dependencies Software composition analysis Vulnerable-dependency requirements Secrets scanning Repo/artifact secret detection Data-protection / config requirements API testing Web-service/API checks ASVS API category Cloud config Posture/benchmark review Cloud Config profile + CASA AL2 infra Mobile MASTG toolchain Reuses existing mobile capability Evidence/case mgmt Workflow + record store The ISO-17025-grade backbone We already operate much of this . The work is integration into a controlled, evidence-producing pipeline , not procurement from zero. The evidence pipeline Build vs. buy - Buy/adopt mature scanners . - Build the thin orchestration + ASVS-mapping + evidence/decision record layer — that's the proprietary, margin-making, audit-satisfying asset. Guardrails - Tool output is input to a human decision , never the decision. - Keep tool versions and configs under control . - Log everything for reproducibility and surveillance audits ."}, {"title": "Phases & Timeline (with gates)", "category": "Program plan", "url": "program-plan-phases-and-timeline.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Phases & Timeline Windows are illustrative for a zero-ISO-17025 start — VERIFY against TrustCB and the accreditation body. The ISO track is the critical path; everything else parallelizes around it. Gantt Phase detail & exit gates P0 — Decide & fund - Approve business case ); commit budget; name executive sponsor + program lead. - Gate: funding + sponsor committed. P1 — Petition & foundations - Submit TrustCB \"Request to become an Authorized Lab\" ; obtain scheme docs . - VERIFY TrustCB fees, timeline, proficiency-eval format. - Select accreditation body + ISO consultant; draft scope statement . - Stand up the ../templates/authorization-tracker.md . - Gate: petition acknowledged; scheme docs in hand; accreditation body engaged; scope drafted. P2 — Build QMS + CASA capability - Build the ISO/IEC 17025 QMS . - Build the CASA method as a controlled document + the evidence/automation pipeline . - Train and competency-record assessors; run dry-run assessments . - Gate: QMS operational with ≥1 internal audit cycle; CASA method validated; ≥2 clean dry-runs. P3 — Trial evaluation, CASA - Perform the TrustCB-overseen trial evaluation for CASA; close any findings. - Gate: TrustCB trial evaluation passed . P4 — ISO/IEC 17025 audit - Accreditation body document review + witnessed assessment ; remediate nonconformities. - Gate: accreditation granted for CASA scope. P5 — Authorization & CASA go-live - TrustCB issues authorization certificate ; ADA lists the lab. - Launch sales + remediation-partner referral program ; deliver first paid CASA engagements + Letters of Validation. - Gate: certificate issued, listed, first revenue booked. P6 — Add profiles - MASA → DASA → Cloud Config . Each: scope extension + profile proficiency/trial evaluation. - Gate per profile: trial eval passed, scope extended, first engagement delivered. P7 — Scale & surveillance - Grow throughput ; sustain surveillance audits and annual revalidations ; track scheme/ASVS changes and re-map. - Gate : surveillance maintained; renewal rate on target. Critical path & compression levers - Critical path = ISO/IEC 17025 . - Compress by: engaging an experienced consultant; deciding scope early; using dry-runs as both QMS records and trial-eval rehearsal; scheduling the witnessed audit and TrustCB trial evaluation close together."}, {"title": "Budget & Financials", "category": "Program plan", "url": "program-plan-budget-and-financials.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Budget & Financials ⚠️ All figures are placeholder RANGES to structure the model — NOT quotes. Replace every line with a real number from TrustCB, the accreditation body, the ISO consultant, and actual salary/tooling costs before any budget approval or investor use. Items tagged VERIFY . Setup cost structure Line item Driver Notes --- --- --- ISO/IEC 17025 consultant Fixed engagement Biggest controllable setup cost; de-risks timeline VERIFY Accreditation body — application + assessment Assessor-days Plus travel for witnessed audit VERIFY TrustCB petition + trial evaluation Per profile Fees + our effort; VERIFY TrustCB fee schedule Tooling / evidence platform Licenses + build Reuse existing scanners; build orchestration layer Initial headcount ramp Salaries during build Quality Manager + Technical Lead + ≥1 assessor before revenue Methodology & QMS build effort Internal time Could be partly internal opex Legal / entity / impartiality structure One-time Independence firewall, contracts Recurring cost structure Line item Driver --- --- Accreditation surveillance Annual assessor-days VERIFY TrustCB authorization maintenance Annual/per-profile VERIFY Assessor + quality salaries Scales with capacity Tooling subscriptions Scanner/platform licenses Sales & marketing Growth-dependent Scheme-update re-mapping ASVS/MASVS version changes P&L sketch Break-even logic Operating break-even ≈ when covers fixed opex . Two things pull break-even earlier: 1. Renewals — year-2 revalidations are low-effort, high-margin and stack on new logos. 2. Automation — cuts assessor-days/job, raising both margin and capacity. Target: operating break-even in ~Year 2 , with setup capital amortized as the moat cost. What to turn into a real model next 1. Get actual fee quotes . VERIFY 2. Set loaded assessor day-rate and assessor-days per assessment by profile/level. 3. Get 3+ competitor price points per profile/level for revenue assumptions. 4. Build a capacity-driven 3-year model ) with scenarios . 5. Derive the funding ask + runway from the trough of cumulative cash."}, {"title": "Organization, Roles & Independence", "category": "Program plan", "url": "program-plan-org-and-roles.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Organization, Roles & Independence Minimum viable org Role Responsibility Notes --- --- --- Executive sponsor Funds, unblocks, owns the business outcome Part-time Lab Director / Program Lead Accountable for authorization + P&L; TrustCB relationship Core hire/owner Quality Manager Owns ISO/IEC 17025 QMS, document/record control, internal audits, management review Effectively mandatory ; dedicated Technical Lead Owns the CASA method, assessor competency, report/decision quality Senior assessor Assessor Execute evaluations; maintain competency records Scale to capacity Impartiality / compliance function Manages conflicts of interest, independence firewall Can be a committee/role, not always a headcount Sales / partnerships Demand + referral channel Can start fractional As profiles are added , extend with profile-specialist assessors — reusing the same QMS and quality function. RACI Activity Director Quality Mgr Tech Lead Assessor Sponsor --- --- --- --- --- --- TrustCB petition & relationship A/R C C — I ISO/IEC 17025 QMS build A R C C I CASA method & validation A C R C I Assessor competency records I R C C — Trial evaluation A C R R I Accreditation audit A R C C I Impartiality / conflict checks A R C I I Sales / partner program A/R I C — C Surveillance & revalidation A R C C I R = Responsible, A = Accountable, C = Consulted, I = Informed. Independence & impartiality ISO/IEC 17025 requires demonstrable impartiality . Concretely: - The lab may not validate an application it built, remediated or consulted on. - Separate \"readiness/advisory\" and \"certifying\" functions with a documented firewall , or refer remediation to partners ). - Maintain a conflict-of-interest register and per-engagement independence checks. - Assessors sign independence/confidentiality attestations per engagement. Competence management - Each assessor has a competency record : training, mentored assessments, authorization to perform specific methods/profiles. - New profiles require re-authorizing assessors for that method. - Competence is auditable — the accreditation body will check it. Build vs. hire - Hire senior for Quality Manager and Technical Lead — these de-risk the two failure modes . - Grow assessors from existing pentest staff + competency program."}, {"title": "Risk Register", "category": "Program plan", "url": "program-plan-risk-register.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Risk Register L = Likelihood, I = Impact . Review monthly; owner acts on mitigation. Risk L I Mitigation Owner --- --- --- --- --- --- R1 ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation slips M H Experienced consultant; decide scope early; parallelize; buffer the timeline Quality Mgr R2 Trial evaluation fails / repeats M H Hire proven assessors; dry-runs vs. ADA Evaluation Methodology; coach before the real eval Tech Lead R3 Scheme changes H M Modular, version-isolated methodology; track official sources; budget re-mapping Tech Lead R4 TrustCB fees/timeline worse than assumed M M VERIFY early ; get written fee schedule before committing budget Program Lead R5 Underestimated setup cost / runway M H Real quotes before approval; scenario model; staged funding tied to gates Sponsor R6 Impartiality breach L H Firewall advisory vs. certify; conflict register; per-engagement checks Quality Mgr R7 Key-person dependency M H Document the QMS; cross-train; competency bench Program Lead R8 Capacity ceiling caps revenue M M Automation to raise throughput/FTE; hire to capacity targets; prioritize high-value AL2 Program Lead R9 Price compression from incumbents M M Lower cost base via automation; win on AL2 depth + bundles + turnaround Sales R10 Demand concentration on Google policy L H Diversify profiles ; watch Google policy; procurement-driven demand Program Lead R11 Roster expands → scarcity erodes M M Move fast; lock reference clients + renewals; brand + region moat Sponsor R12 Quality incident / invalid validation L H QA/independent review; retained evidence; reproducible decisions; surveillance readiness Quality Mgr R13 Client data handling / confidentiality breach L H ISO-grade data controls; least-privilege access; never retain client secrets in the repo Quality Mgr The three that most threaten the thesis 1. R1 — it's the critical path; every week here delays revenue. 2. R2 — gates authorization; competence is the mitigation. 3. R5 — kills the business case if numbers are wrong → VERIFY first . Watchlist - OWASP ASVS 5.x adoption cadence in CASA. - ADA/Google policy changes to CASA scope/assurance mapping. - New authorized labs added to the roster ."}, {"title": "ADA Programs — Overview & Mandate Map (internal reference)", "category": "Reference", "url": "reference-ada-programs-overview.html", "lang": "en", "text": "ADA Programs — Overview & Mandate Map What every App Defense Alliance program is, who stands behind it, and which ones a testing lab can actually deliver. Research date 2026-10-27; items tagged VERIFY . Per-program detail pages are linked in the last column. The ecosystem in one paragraph The App Defense Alliance is hosted by the Linux Foundation and is backed by Google, Meta, and Microsoft . It publishes standardized app-security assessment programs delivered by Authorized Labs , runs a public Certified Products Portal , and hosts an industry Malware Mitigation collaboration. AI and desktop application assessments are under development . The concrete commercial mandate today runs through Google ; Meta and Microsoft back the Alliance and bring adjacent programs. Master mandate table Program / Framework What it secures Who mandates / backs it Authorized testing body / how delivered Detail page --- --- --- --- --- CASA Web applications & APIs Google ; ADA backers Google · Meta · Microsoft ADA Authorized Labs CASA MASA Mobile apps Google Play ADA Authorized Labs MASA DASA Developer-attested app security posture ADA ADA Authorized Labs validate the attestation VERIFY DASA Cloud Config Cloud environment configuration posture ADA ADA Authorized Labs Cloud Config AI Agents & Tools AI agents / LLM tools ADA — under development VERIFY Future ADA Authorized Labs AI Agents Desktop App Assessment Desktop applications ADA — under development VERIFY Future ADA Authorized Labs Malware Mitigation Threat-intel sharing to block malicious apps ADA collaboration Security-vendor collaboration — not a lab assessment service Certified Products Portal Public registry of ADA-certified apps ADA / Linux Foundation Registry Lab-deliverable vs. not. A testing lab earns revenue from CASA, MASA, DASA, Cloud Config . Malware Mitigation and the Certified Products Portal are not lab services — they're an industry collaboration and a registry; include them for completeness, not as revenue lines. Emerging programs - AI Agents & Tools — the highest-upside emerging area given the AI-agent + OAuth-scope wave. Build methodology and thought leadership now; formalize when ADA publishes the profile. Full note: ../ada-authorization/ai-agents-profile.md . - Desktop application assessment — announced as under development; scope/standard TBD. VERIFY before planning. Cross-ecosystem adjacencies ADA is backed by Google, Meta and Microsoft, but each also runs its own app-trust program on a different scheme . These broaden the narrative TAM and create cross-sell, but do not run on the ADA authorized-lab "}, {"title": "Profiles at a glance", "category": "Reference", "url": "reference-profiles-matrix.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Profiles at a glance CASA MASA DASA Cloud Config --- --- --- --- --- Target Web apps & APIs Mobile apps Developer-attested posture Cloud environment config Standard basis OWASP ASVS OWASP MASVS/MASTG ADA scheme ADA scheme + CIS/provider benchmarks VERIFY Commercial driver Google restricted OAuth scopes ; Workspace Marketplace Google Play \"independent security review\" badge Lighter-touch / volume Bundles with CASA AL2 Assurance levels AL1 , AL2 Per MASA scheme Attestation-validation Config review Deliverable Letter of Validation Play-referenceable validation Scheme validation Findings + validation Revalidation Annual Periodic VERIFY VERIFY VERIFY Our sequence 1 2 3 4 Internal synergy Web/API pentest Existing MASTG capability CASA base reuse Existing cloud pentest/config Each profile is authorized separately . All share the same ISO/IEC 17025 QMS, evidence pipeline and quality function → low marginal cost to add after the first. VERIFY every cell against the current TrustCB scheme documents and ADA pages — profile definitions, standards versions, assurance mappings and revalidation windows all change."}, {"title": "Glossary", "category": "Reference", "url": "reference-glossary.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Glossary - ADA — App Defense Alliance. Industry body running standardized app-security assessment programs . - CASA — Cloud Application Security Assessment. ADA's web-app/API assessment, derived from OWASP ASVS; required by Google for apps using sensitive/restricted OAuth scopes; annually revalidated . - MASA — Mobile Application Security Assessment. ADA's mobile assessment, based on OWASP MASVS/MASTG; supports the Google Play \"independent security review\" badge. - DASA — Developer-Attested Security Assessment. Lighter-touch ADA profile centered on developer attestation validated by a lab. VERIFY exact definition. - Cloud Config. ADA profile reviewing cloud environment security configuration. - Assurance Level . Current CASA levels, both \"Lab Tested – Lab Verified.\" AL1 = evidence verification; AL2 = comprehensive test incl. deployment infra + data storage. The framework user sets the required level. - Tier 1/2/3. Legacy CASA naming still seen on older certificates; mapped to AL0/AL1/AL2. - LOV — Letter of Validation. The deliverable an authorized lab issues on a successful assessment . - Authorized Lab. A lab TrustCB has authorized to perform ADA assessments; requires ISO/IEC 17025 + proficiency evaluation. - TrustCB. The external certification body ADA uses to manage lab authorization, review profile assessments, and issue the authorization certificate. - ISO/IEC 17025. International standard for the competence of testing and calibration laboratories — the gating accreditation for an authorized lab. - Accreditation body. The ILAC-MRA-signatory national/regional body that grants ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation . - ILAC MRA. International mutual-recognition arrangement making accreditation internationally recognized. - Proficiency / trial evaluation. A TrustCB-overseen supervised assessment proving the lab can correctly execute a profile's methodology before authorization. - QMS — Quality Management System. The documented system satisfying ISO/IEC 17025 . - Surveillance. Periodic re-assessment by the accreditation body to maintain accreditation. - Impartiality. ISO/IEC 17025 requirement; a lab cannot certify work it built/consulted on. - OWASP ASVS. Application Security Verification Standard — the control basis for CASA . - OWASP MASVS / MASTG. Mobile verification standard + testing guide — basis for MASA. - Restricted / sensitive OAuth scopes. Google API permissions whose use triggers the CASA requirement."}, {"title": "Official Sources & What to Verify", "category": "Reference", "url": "reference-official-sources.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Official Sources & What to Verify Treat only the primary sources below as authoritative. Vendor blogs and this repo are working interpretations. Re-check before any commitment — the scheme changes. Primary Source Use --- --- ADA — Authorized Labs <br https://www.appdefensealliance.org/certification/authorized-labs The 5 official steps, ISO/IEC 17025 + proficiency requirement, profiles list, current lab roster, scheme-doc links ADA — CASA standard <br https://www.appdefensealliance.org/standards/casa CASA program definition ADA — CASA tiering / assurance levels <br https://appdefensealliance.dev/casa/casa-tiering AL1/AL2 definitions; who sets the level; annual revalidation ADA — CASA requirements <br https://appdefensealliance.dev/casa/casa-requirements The current CASA requirement set + ASVS version/count TrustCB — ADA certification <br https://www.trustcb.com/ Lab authorization process, petition intake, fees, scheme documents ADA scheme documents Security Test Lab Authorization , Scheme Overview , Evaluation Methodology OWASP ASVS <br https://owasp.org/www-project-asvs/ The standard under CASA OWASP MASVS / MASTG <br https://mas.owasp.org/ The standard under MASA ISO/IEC 17025 + national accreditation body Accreditation requirements + local process ILAC <br https://ilac.org/ Accreditation-body mutual recognition Must-verify list - TrustCB fee schedule . - Exact proficiency / trial-evaluation format per profile. - Whether a specific accreditation body is required or any ILAC-MRA signatory is accepted. - Current CASA requirement set + ASVS version and count. - Current profile catalogue and each profile's methodology document . - AL1/AL2 scope + badge mapping . - Revalidation windows per profile. - Current authorized-lab roster . - Accreditation timeline + cost from the chosen body + consultant. Source hygiene - Figures in this repo tagged VERIFY came from secondary/vendor sources or were illustrative — replace with primary data. - The tier → assurance-level rename and the Linux Foundation / TrustCB model are recent; older pages/certificates may show stale terminology. - Research performed 2026-10-27 ; re-confirm anything time-sensitive."}, {"title": "Authorization Tracker (live checklist)", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-authorization-tracker.html", "lang": "en", "text": "Authorization Tracker Single source of truth for \"where are we?\" across both tracks. Update weekly. Status summary Status Owner Target date Notes --- --- --- --- --- Business case approved ☐ Sponsor TrustCB petition submitted ☐ Program Lead Scheme docs obtained ☐ Program Lead Security Test Lab Auth / Scheme Overview / Eval Methodology Fees & timeline VERIFY ☐ Program Lead Written confirmation from TrustCB Accreditation body selected ☐ Quality Mgr ISO consultant engaged ☐ Quality Mgr Scope statement drafted ☐ Quality Mgr Track A — ISO/IEC 17025 - Scope defined - QMS: impartiality & confidentiality controls - QMS: personnel competence / records - QMS: document & record control - QMS: method validation - QMS: nonconforming work / corrective action / complaints - Internal audit cycle completed - Management review completed - Dry-run assessments with records - Accreditation body document review - Witnessed assessment - Nonconformities closed - Accreditation granted ★ Track B — ADA / TrustCB CASA - Method built as controlled document - Evidence/automation pipeline operational - Assessors authorized - Trial evaluation scheduled - Trial evaluation passed ★ - Authorization certificate issued - Listed by ADA - First paid engagement + LOV delivered MASA - Scope extension filed - MASTG method as controlled document - Assessors authorized - Trial evaluation passed ★ - Authorization issued / listed DASA - Methodology defined from scheme docs - Trial evaluation passed ★ - Authorization issued / listed Cloud Config - Methodology defined from scheme docs - Trial evaluation passed ★ - Authorization issued / listed Ongoing - Surveillance calendar set - Annual revalidation process live - Scheme/ASVS change-watch assigned - Impartiality / conflict register maintained"}];